{{!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     navbar/jsonld — SiteNavigationElement for the main menu.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Rendered through partials/navigation.hbs, from the same {{navigation}}
     helper the visible bar uses. That is the point: the structured data and
     the markup cannot describe different menus, because there is one source
     and one loop.

     ── {{json}} DOES THE ESCAPING, AND IT HAS TO ────────────────────────────
     Handlebars' own {{ }} escapes for HTML — an apostrophe becomes &#x27; —
     which is exactly wrong inside a <script>: JSON-LD is parsed as JSON, not
     as HTML, so an HTML entity lands in the string literally. Ghost's {{json}}
     helper serialises properly AND escapes <, > and & to \\u003C-style
     sequences, which is what stops a label containing "</script>" from ending
     the block early. Never hand-quote a value in here.

     ── TOP-LEVEL ITEMS ONLY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Children are skipped on purpose. SiteNavigationElement describes a site's
     main sections, and the leading "-" is a PRESENTATION convention for
     Ghost's flat menu — it must not leak into structured data, and stripping
     it would mean rebuilding the label as a JSON string by hand, which is the
     one thing the paragraph above says not to do.

     ⚠ It is emitted in <head>, so it must not be inside the <nav>. Google
     accepts it anywhere in the document, but a <script> in the body is a
     parse pause the head does not have.

     ── WHY THE URL IS NOT {{json url}} ──────────────────────────────────────
     The raw `url` property is whatever was typed in Admin — usually relative
     ("/about/"), and structured data needs absolute. The {{url}} HELPER
     resolves that, but it returns a Handlebars SafeString, and {{json}} would
     serialise the OBJECT ({"string":"…"}) rather than its text. So the URL is
     quoted by hand, in a TRIPLE stash: a double stash would HTML-escape an
     "&" in a query string to "&amp;", which lands in the JSON literally and
     ships a wrong URL. A quote character cannot appear unencoded in a URL, so
     the JSON stays valid. This reasoning does NOT extend to `name` — arbitrary
     label text is exactly what {{json}} is for.

     ⚠ KEEP THE CLOSING BRACE ON ITS OWN LINE. Written inline, the object's
     `}` lands directly after `{{json url}}` — and `}}}` is a triple-stash to
     Handlebars, not a mustache followed by a brace. The template then fails
     to COMPILE, which takes the whole site to a 500; it is not a rendering
     glitch confined to this block. The line breaks are load-bearing.
     ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ --}}
{{~#if navigation~}}
<script type="application/ld+json">
{"@context": "https://schema.org", "@graph": [
{{~#foreach navigation~}}
  {{~#match label "~^" "-"}}{{else}}
    {{~#unless @first}},{{/unless}}
    {
      "@type": "SiteNavigationElement",
      "position": {{@number}},
      "name": {{json label}},
      "url": "{{{url absolute="true"}}}"
    }
  {{~/match~}}
{{~/foreach~}}
]}
</script>
{{~/if~}}
